---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Screenshot from '../components/Screenshot.astro';
import Limits from '../components/Limits.astro';
import { REPO_URL } from '../site';
---

<Base
  fullTitle="Open-source Thunderbird alternative with fast search · OpenWren"
  description="OpenWren is a free, open-source Thunderbird alternative for Windows, Mac and Linux: a modern interface, mail encrypted on disk, and one fast search box."
>
  <section class="page-intro">
    <h1>An open-source Thunderbird alternative</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Thunderbird is the reason a free, open-source desktop mail program still exists, and for many people it is the
      right one. OpenWren is for those who want less: mail only, a modern interface, storage that is encrypted, and one
      search box that is quick and covers everything.
    </p>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="button" href="/download/">Download OpenWren</a>
      <a class="button secondary" href="/search/">How search works</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="page-body">
    <Screenshot src="inbox.png" alt="The OpenWren inbox: folders, the message list and an open message" label="Inbox" width={760} />

    <h2>Where the two differ</h2>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th></th><th>OpenWren</th><th>Thunderbird</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Open source, free</td><td>Yes (Apache 2.0)</td><td>Yes (MPL 2.0)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Windows, Mac, Linux</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes, and Android</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Gmail and IMAP</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
          <tr><td>POP3, Exchange, Microsoft 365</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Search</td><td>One box: all accounts, sender, subject and text, with operators</td><td>Quick filter for the current folder, plus a separate global search</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Mail on disk</td><td>Encrypted database</td><td>Ordinary files in your profile</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Tracking pixels</td><td>Removed; remote images blocked</td><td>Remote images blocked</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Tracking codes in links</td><td>Removed</td><td>Kept</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Calendar, contacts, tasks, feeds, chat</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Add-ons and themes</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
          <tr><td>OpenPGP and S/MIME</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Age and backing</td><td>New in 2026, one maintainer</td><td>More than twenty years, a funded team</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="muted">
      To the best of my knowledge in November 2026. If a cell is wrong or out of date, please
      <a href={`${REPO_URL}/issues`}>say so</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Search</h2>
    <p>
      If one thing sends Thunderbird users looking elsewhere, it is search: the quick filter looks only in the folder
      you are in, the global search opens a different view with its own rules, and on a large profile it can take
      seconds.
    </p>
    <p>
      OpenWren has one search box, opened with <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>K</kbd>. It looks through the sender, recipients,
      subject and text of all mail in all accounts, shows the results newest first and grouped by year, and can be
      narrowed with buttons or with operators such as <code>from:</code> and <code>before:2024-01-31</code>. On an
      encrypted mailbox of 200,000 messages a typical search takes about 30 ms, and the slowest case, a word found in
      most of the mailbox, about 190 ms. The <a href="/benchmarks/">benchmarks</a> give the method; no other program
      was measured, so there are no comparison numbers here.
    </p>
    <p>It has limits too: results are by date and not by relevance, and it does not correct typing mistakes. The <a href="/search/">search page</a> lists them.</p>

    <h2>Stay with Thunderbird if</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>you need a calendar, an address book, feeds or chat in the same program;</li>
      <li>you use Exchange, Microsoft 365 or POP3;</li>
      <li>you send or receive PGP or S/MIME encrypted mail;</li>
      <li>you depend on add-ons, or on message filters you built over the years.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Try OpenWren if</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>your accounts are Gmail or IMAP and you want mail only;</li>
      <li>search is what you use most and you want it quick and in one place;</li>
      <li>you want your mail and its search index encrypted on disk without setting up disk encryption yourself;</li>
      <li>you want a small program: a 14 MB download that starts in about 0.6 s.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      You can run both on the same IMAP account while you decide. Nothing needs exporting: OpenWren downloads your
      folders and mail from the server.
    </p>

    <h2>What OpenWren does not do</h2>
    <Limits />
  </section>
</Base>
